ooking at the data:\n# Section 1957: months show data for 1957, 1959, 1961 in that order\n# Section 1958: months show data for 1958, 1960, 1966 in that order\n# etc.\n\n# But I need to figure out the exact mapping. Let me look at the header row.\nheader = re.search(r'<th>Period</th>.*?<th>Treasury bonds.*?<th>Moody.*?</tr>', content, re.DOTALL)\nif header:\n    print(\"Header structure:\")\n    # Find all the year headers in the monthly series section\n    # The pattern shows multiple years per row\n\n# Actually, let me use a simpler approach - I know the structure from looking at the table:\n# Looking at the text version output earlier:\n\n# For 1957 section (first block):\n# - 1957: Jan-Dec values in first two columns\n# - 1959: in columns 4-5 (after empty td)\n# - 1961: in columns 6-7\n\n# For 1958 section:\n# - 1958: in first two columns  \n# - 1960: in columns 4-5\n# - 1966: in columns 6-7\n\n# But we need all 1957-1968. Let me trace through each row systematically.\n\n# Let's extract all June data by parsing row by row\njune_data = []  # (year, yield)\n\n# Track what year each column pair represents as we go\n# Looking at the structure: each section has 3 year columns\n\ncurrent_section = None\ncolumn_years = []  # [year1, year2, year3] for the 3 column pairs\n\n# Split content into lines for easier parsing\nrows = content.split('</tr><tr>')\n\n# Let me just manually map this based on visual inspection of the table:\n# Looking at the first \"June\" row: <td>June</td><td>3.58</td><td>3.91</td><td></td><td>3.88</td><td>4.46</td><td></td><td>4.00</td><td>4.23</td>\n# This row corresponds to the 1957 section, with columns for 1957, 1959, 1961\n# Values: 3.58 (1957 Treasury), 3.91 (1957 Moody's), 3.88 (1959 Treasury), 4.46 (1959 Moody's), 4.00 (1961 Treasury), 4.23 (1961 Moody's)\n\n# And the second June row (in 1958 section):\n# <td>June</td><td>3.20</td><td>3.57</td><td>1964</td><td>4.28</td><td>4.45</td><td></td><td>4.63</td><td>5.07</td>\n# Wait, this has \"196