{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775346190-wYXkJNy6HQJ6rzVwGabT","role":"assistant","created":1775346207,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand! The table structure is:\n\n- Section 1957: Contains data for 1957 (cols 1-2), 1959 (cols 4-5), 1961 (cols 7-8)\n- Section 1958: Contains data for 1958 (cols 1-2), 1960 (cols 4-5), 1966 (cols 7-8)\n- etc.\n\nThe years are shown as headers in each row (like \"1959\", \"1961\" in row 2).\n\nSo for June in each year:\n- 1957 June: First column pair = 3.58\n- 1959 June: Third column pair from row with \"June\" in 1957 section = 3.88\n- 1961 June: Fifth column pair from row with \"June\" in 1957 section = 4.00\n\n- 1958 June: First column pair in 1958 section = 3.20\n- 1960 June: Third column pair from row with \"June\" in 1958 section = 4.08\n- 1966 June: Fifth column pair from row with \"June\" in 1958 section = 4.63\n\nNow I need to continue and find all the June yields from 1957-1968. Let me parse this properly.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_61068e4e0357461c825d5907","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\nimport re\n\nwith open('/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1968_07_page_83.txt', 'r') as f:\n    content = f.read()\n\n# Now I understand the structure - let's extract all June yields properly\n# Section headers give the first year, and other years appear in the rows\n\n# Let me build a mapping of year -> June Treasury bond yield\n\njune_yields = {}\n\n# Split into sections by year section headers\nsections = content.split('<tr><td colspan=\"9\">')\n\n# For each section, determine which years' data it contains\n# Based on the pattern we observed:\n# Section after \"1957\" header: 1957, 1959, 1961\n# Section after \"1958\" header: 1958, 1960, 1966\n\n# Let me parse by looking at which years appear in each section block\n\n# Better approach: for each row, find the June Treasury bond yield\n# and figure out which year it belongs to from context\n\n# Let's extract all June data with their column positions\n# and then map based on the known structure\n\nmonthly_section = content[content.find('Monthly series'):]\nrows = monthly_section.split('</tr><tr>')\n\n# Track current year for first column pair\n# Based on observed pattern:\nsection_mapping = {\n    '1957': [1957, 1959, 1961],\n    '1958': [1958, 1960, 1966],\n    # Continue based on pattern in the data\n}\n\n# Let me find all the section headers (year blocks)\nyear_sections = {}\ncurrent_first_year = None\n\nfor row in rows:\n    # Check for section header like <td colspan=\"9\">1957</td>\n    header_match = re.search(r'<td colspan=\"9\">(\\d{4})</td>', row)\n    if header_match:\n        current_first_year = int(header_match.group(1))\n        year_sections[current_first_year] = []\n        continue\n    \n    # When we see a row with month names and year indicators, track it\n    # Look for pattern: <td>Month</td><td>value</td>...\n    month_match = re.search(r'<td>(January|February|March|April|May|June|July|August|September|October|November|December)</td>', row)\n    if month_match and current_first_year:\n        month = month_match.group(1)\n        if month == 'June':\n            # Extract all numeric values (Treasury yields, ignoring Moody's)\n            # The pattern is: Month, Treasury1, Moodys1, [Year2,] Treasury2, Moodys2, [Year3,] Treasury3, Moodys3\n            # Actually let's be more precise\n            \n            # Get all values from this row\n            all_cells = re.findall(r'<td>([\\d.]+)\\s*(?:2/)?</td>', row)\n            # Get year markers\n            year_markers = re.findall(r'<td>(\\d{4})</td>', row)\n            \n            print(f\"Year section: {current_first_year}\")\n            print(f\"  Year markers in row: {year_markers}\")\n            print(f\"  All yield values: {all_cells}\")\n            print()\n\n# Now I understand the pattern better. Let me build the mapping.\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"g