appear from the first few rows\n\n# Actually, the simpler way is to look at the explicit year markers in each row\n# And assume the first column pair is always the section base year\n\n# Let me extract ALL unique years we see in the table:\nall_years_in_table = set()\nfor m in re.finditer(r'<td>(\\d{4})</td>', monthly_section):\n    year = int(m.group(1))\n    if 1950 <= year <= 1970:\n        all_years_in_table.add(year)\n\nprint(\"All years appearing in monthly table:\", sorted(all_years_in_table))\nprint()\n\n# Now let me extract June yields for each year by analyzing the pattern more carefully\n# I'll build a mapping by checking which years appear in each month's row\n\n# For each June row, I need to figure out the year for each column pair\n# The pattern seems to be:\n# - If section starts with year X, first column pair is for X\n# - Then years are listed in subsequent column pairs if present\n\n# Actually wait - I just realized the table might be showing DIFFERENT years\n# in each row block. Let me look at the raw table header structure again\n\n# The table shows yearly columns as: 1957 | 1959, 1961 | 1958 | 1960, 1966 | ...\n# This is getting complex. Let me try a different approach.\n\n# Let me manually build the mapping from what I can observe:\n# Section 1957 (header shows 1957): Contains data for 1957, 1959, 1961\n# Section 1958 (header shows 1958): Contains data for 1958, 1960, 1966\n\n# Looking at June data:\n# June 1957 section: yields are [3.58, 3.88, 4.00] for Treasury (columns 1, 3, 5)\n# June 1958 section: yields are [3.20, ?, 4.63] - need to find the middle one\n\n# Wait, I should check by looking at the DECEMBER row which has explicit year markers!\n\n# December 1957 section: <td>December</td><td>3.30 2/</td><td>3.81</td><td>1962</td><td>4.06</td><td>4.58</td><td>1968</td><td>4.34</td><td>4.33</td>\n# This gives us: 1957->3.30, 1962->4.06, 1968->4.34\n\n# Wait, that doesn't match the earlier section interpretation!\n# Let me reconsider the struct