{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775346353-YjtZtmI7g2p3PUvYcaE7","role":"assistant","created":1775346374,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to carefully map the yields to years. Let me reconsider the table structure.\n\nLooking at the first June row (from the \"1957\" section):\n- Yields at positions: [(1, 3.58), (2, 3.91), (4, 3.88), (5, 4.46), (7, 4.0), (8, 4.23)]\n- This gives: Treasury/Moody's pairs at positions (1,2), (4,5), (7,8)\n- So: Treasury yields at positions 1, 4, 7 -> values 3.58, 3.88, 4.00\n\nAnd from the earlier January row in 1957 section:\n'<td>January</td><td>3.34</td><td>3.77</td><td>1959</td><td>3.91 2/</td><td>4.12</td><td>1961</td><td>3.89</td><td>4.32</td>'\n\nThis shows explicit years in the row! 1959 appears at position 3, 1961 appears at position 6.\n\nSo for the January row in 1957 section:\n- Position 1 (Treasury): 3.34 for year 1957 (the section header year)\n- Position 4 (Treasury): 3.91 for year 1959 (explicitly marked)\n- Position 7 (Treasury): 3.89 for year 1961 (explicitly marked)\n\nThis confirms the mapping:\n- First year in section = section header year (1957 in this case)\n- Subsequent years are explicitly marked in the row\n\nNow for the June row in 1957 section:\n- Position 1 (Treasury): 3.58 for year 1957\n- Position 4 (Treasury): 3.88 for year (need to infer - likely 1959 based on pattern in other months)\n- Position 7 (Treasury): 4.00 for year (need to infer - likely 1961 based on pattern)\n\n\n- The January row confirms this structure, so I can apply the same logic to June.\n\nLooking at the second June row, I see a year marker for 1964 at position 3, which means position 1 corresponds to 1958, position 4 to 1964, and position 7 should be the next year in the sequence. The Treasury yields at these positions are 3.20, 4.28, and 4.63 respectively. The table organizes years in pairs across columns, with each row representing a month and the year markers indicating which years appear in that particular row.\n\nFrom the January data in the 1958 section, the year sequence is 1958, 1960, and 1966. This suggests the pattern increments by 2 for the first two years after the base year, then jumps significantly for the third year. Applying this to June gives me yields of 3.20 for 1958 and 4.28 for the year at position 4.\n\nLooking at the row structure more carefully, the year markers appear in a specific pattern within the table columns. Since there's no year marker before position 1, it defaults to the section header year (1957), and the lack of a marker at position 6 indicates position 7 maps to the next year in the sequence. With only two June rows available in the 1957-1968 range, I need to cross-reference the August 1968 bulletin to identify what data that issue contains.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_e06f13580e7b4a4e963c9597","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\nimport re\n\n# Let me understand the mapping better by looking at the January row in each section\n# to see the explicit year markers\n\nwith open('/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1968_07_page_83.txt', 'r') as f:\n    content = f.read()\n\nmonthly_section = content[content.find('Monthly series'):]\nrows = monthly_section.split('</tr><tr>')\n\n# For each section header, check the January row to see year markers\n# This will tell us what years' data is in each column pair\n\ncurrent_section = None\n\nfor row in rows:\n    # Check for section header\n    if '<td colspan=\"9\">' in row:\n        section_match = re.search(r'<td colspan=\"9\">(\\d{4})</td>', row)\n        if section_match:\n            current_section = int(section_match.group(1))\n            print(f\"\\n=== Section {current_section} ===\")\n    \n    # Check for January row\n    if '<td>January</td>' in row and current_section:\n        # Get all cells\n        cells = re.findall(r'<td(.*?)>(.*?)</td>', row)\n        \n        print(f\"January row in section {current_section}:\")\n        for i, (attr, val) in enumerate(cells):\n            val = val.strip()\n            print(f\"  Position {i}: {val}\")\n        print()\n\n# Now I understand the table structure:\n# Section 1957: Column pairs are for years [1957, 1959, 1961]\n# Section 1958: Column pairs are for years [1958, 1960, 1966]\n\n# But wait, I need data for ALL years from 1957-1968, not just these!\n# The table only shows SOME years in each section\n# Let me find which years are covered in which sections\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
